[Music]  for morning rounds our look at the  medical news of the week first up we're  in the warmer months time when ticks are  at their most active and that of course  means more cases of Lyme disease it is  the most commonly reported vector borne  illness in the country meaning illnesses  transmitted from animals or insects to  humans according to the Centers for  Disease Control there are around 30,000  cases reported each year although the  actual number is likely to be much  higher in 2015 95 percent of these cases  were reported in 14 states a majority in  the Northeast and New England regions a  new study out this week in the Journal  of medical entomology examined a new  type of bacteria that can cause Lyme  disease here now to talk about this CBS  News chief medical correspondent dr. Jon  LaPook and CBS News contributor dr. Tara  Narula so John give us some idea what  did the study look at him what were the  main points that they found so here's  what they were trying to figure out how  long does this new species have to be at  does the tick carrying this new species  have to be attached to you in order to  transmit it the reason why that's  important is that the typical species  the tick has to be attached 36 to 48  hours before it gets transmitted so it's  very important to see is it the same  thing for this new species and they did  studies in mice and they found that yes  thankfully it has to be typically more  than 48 hours for there to be a  significant chance of transmission it  can happen rarely before then why is  that important well when my patients  call me which they did this past weekend  and say they have a tick on me one of  the first questions I ask is how long  was it attached to you if it was  attached for 36 to 48 hours or less than  36 hours then the odds of them getting  Lyme disease from it are very low and so  if it's more than that then there are  various strategies with antibiotics and  watching people for symptoms that can be  used to try to figure out whether you  should treat or not treat so Tara this  obviously now is the time and more and  more people are spending more and more  time outside so what kind of tips are  there for folks who are now hitting the  hiking trails right now I was just  thinking about this this weekend in  Connecticut when I was walking with my  five-year-old outside so you clearly  want to be outdoors but you can take  measures and be  particularly in these warmer months so  you want to stay in the center of trails  and really avoid those kind of high tall  grassy bushy wooded areas you want to  make sure when you come indoors you do a  full-body tick check you want to be  there shower typically within two hours  of coming in check your kids check your  pets check your gear make sure to use  repellent at least 20% DEET you can buy  permethrin treated clothing or spray  that on your clothing in gear and then  one other tip that John tonight we're  talking about is to tumble dry your  clothes when you come in on a high  setting for about 10 minutes and that  should kill any ticks the other thing  that the ticks are not shy everywhere  including where the moon don't shine yes  they like to crawl I enough said you  really have to look at every millimeter  of no really and no matter how much  prevention there is some are gonna get  through and there's so many different  symptoms give us some idea of the major  ones that people come up but one of the  things is that you know you get this  classic rash the rash of Lyme it can be  anywhere from 3 to 30 days it's  typically within a week or two not  everybody has that that rash you can  have aches and pains a flu-like illness  fever headache so if somebody seems so  normal that they see other and there are  a lot of times that people have these  viruses and it's it has nothing to do  with Lyme but I think a high level of  suspicion is very important and then  there are some other symptoms like you  can have neurological symptoms  especially later you can have a Bell's  palsy  we have drooping of one side of the face  you can have late symptoms you and we've  heard about chronic Lyme and and all of  that so I think the bottom line here is  you want to have a very high index of  suspicion especially if you're in an  area where you know that there's Lyme  right our next topic is equipping  yourself to hear better a newly  published research letter in the Journal  of American Medical Association compared  personal sound amplification products  with traditional hearing aids also known  as PSA PS they are less expensive than  hearing aids and can be purchased over  the counter however the devices are not  specifically labeled for hearing loss  treatment like conventional hearing aids  testing was done involving a group of 42  adults with an average age of 71 so  tarrying comparing these sort of new  devices with traditional hearing aids  what what did the research find well the  that we really need more low-cost  accessible options for people and that's  what these PSAP s are offering right now  and so when they compared them they  found that unaided speech understanding  accuracy was about 76% with hearing aids  that improved to about 88 percent with  the PSAP s that they looked at it  improved to about 80 percent so there  was some improvement but not to the  degree that it improved with hearing  aids in fact one of the five PS CPS they  tested what had worst speech accuracy  understanding so again this was a small  group of people it was only five  products the PSAP s that they tested so  we need a lot more research into this  and certainly more options for people  and it's actually not just the medical  community that's looking at it there's  bipartisan legislation surrounding  hearing aids right now yeah and it  they're looking at it right now and the  reason why that's so important is almost  50 million Americans have some degree of  hearing loss what getting behind apple  pie is yeah it's really easy less less  than 20% of people with these hearing  problems are actually having any kind of  hearing device something to help them  hear and the reason is it's expensive so  for Medicare for example you can it's  covered for you to go to an audiologist  an expert and say okay you need a  hearing aid but the hearing aid itself  which could be more than two thousand  dollars that's not covered so something  needs to be done yeah well let's finally  go to our last topic Medical Solutions  from the see a study published by the  American Society of microbiology looked  at the research for tuberculosis  treatments in ocean life a team of  researchers at the University of Central  Florida screen more than 4,000 chemical  extracts from ocean creatures like  sponges to see if they could find any  that might be effective against the  disease in fact they turned up 26  compounds that were effective against  replicating bacteria but they know they  have a lot more work to do studying them  in detail so what is all of this mean  well so many of our medications come  from plants yet another reason to  peacefully coexist good nature yes not  destroy them why did it like we needed  other reason doctors Jon LaPook and Tara  Narula thank you

